  {"name": "Led", "kind": "type", "category": "program", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["vec3 shade(Led led)"], "ty": "", "summary": "the per-LED input struct passed to shade()", "doc": "The type of shade()'s parameter. It is not a real value: `led` names the per-LED inputs (`led.pos`, `led.s`, ...), which compile to direct context loads. Only shade() takes it.", "example": "vec3 shade(Led led) { return vec3(led.s); }", "related": ["led", "shade"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "int8", "kind": "type", "category": "storage", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["buffer int8 name;", "texture int8 name(w, h);"], "ty": "", "summary": "buffer/texture element stored as a signed byte, read back as int", "doc": "A storage-only element type for buffers and textures: each element takes one byte (-128..127) and reads back as an `int`. It cannot be used for locals, state or uniforms.", "example": "buffer int8 hits;\nvec3 shade(Led led) {\n  int h = hits[led.idx];\n  hits[led.idx] = 1;\n  return vec3(float(h));\n}", "related": ["int16", "buffer", "int"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "int16", "kind": "type", "category": "storage", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["buffer int16 name;", "texture int16 name(w, h);"], "ty": "", "summary": "buffer/texture element stored as a signed 16-bit integer, read back as int", "doc": "A storage-only element type for buffers and textures: two bytes per element (-32768..32767), read back as an `int`. It cannot be used for locals, state or uniforms.", "example": "buffer int16 age;\nvec3 shade(Led led) {\n  int a = age[led.idx] + 1;\n  age[led.idx] = a;\n  return vec3(0.0);\n}", "related": ["int8", "buffer", "int"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "trigger", "kind": "type", "category": "trigger", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["uniform trigger name;", "uniform trigger name \"label\";", "export trigger name;"], "ty": "", "summary": "an event counter: `uniform trigger tap;` + `fired(tap)` in update(); a driver bumps `export trigger` with fire()", "doc": "A trigger carries discrete events (a tap, a button press) rather than a level. An effect declares `uniform trigger tap;` and asks `fired(tap)` in update(), which is true once per event. A sensor driver declares `export trigger tap;` and calls `fire(tap)`; the uniform pool connects the two by name, and the app shows a trigger as a button. Under the hood it is a float counter.", "example": "uniform trigger tap;\nstate float flash;\nvoid update() {\n  flash = max(flash - dt * 2.0, 0.0);\n  if (fired(tap)) { flash = 1.0; }\n}\nvec3 shade(Led led) { return vec3(flash); }", "related": ["fired", "fire", "uniform", "export"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "time", "kind": "context", "category": "input", "scope": "any", "sigs": [], "ty": "float", "summary": "seconds since effect start (float)", "doc": "Seconds since the effect started. Fixtures aligned with `align_time` share it. Use it for motion with a constant rate (`fract(led.s - time)`, `sin(time * 0.7)`), never multiplied by a rate uniform: `time * speed` jumps when the slider moves. Integrate rate uniforms in update() with `dt` instead.", "example": "float wave = fract(led.s - time * 0.25);", "related": ["dt", "frame", "update"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "dt", "kind": "context", "category": "input", "scope": "any", "sigs": [], "ty": "float", "summary": "seconds since last frame (float)", "doc": "Seconds since the previous frame (the device measures it; the preview steps it). The building block of every rate control: `ph = fract(ph + dt * speed)` in update() advances a phase smoothly however the knob moves.", "example": "uniform float speed : 0.0 .. 4.0 = 1.0;\nstate float ph;\nvoid update() { ph = fract(ph + dt * speed); }\nvec3 shade(Led led) { return vec3(fract(led.s + ph)); }", "related": ["time", "update", "state"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "frame", "kind": "context", "category": "input", "scope": "any", "sigs": [], "ty": "float", "summary": "frame counter (a float holding an integer)", "doc": "The number of frames since the effect started, as a float holding a whole number. Handy as a seed (`hash(frame)`) for per-frame randomness.", "example": "float flicker = hash(frame + float(led.idx));", "related": ["time", "hash"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "led", "kind": "context", "category": "input", "scope": "any", "sigs": [], "ty": "Led", "summary": "the per-LED input struct (also the shade() parameter)", "doc": "The current LED's inputs, valid in shade() and its helpers: position (`led.pos`, `led.room`), index and count, topology (`led.seg`, `led.s`, `led.dist`, `led.branch`) and texture coordinate (`led.uv`). Reading them as `fixed(led.s)` loads a fixed-point copy directly, with no conversion.", "example": "vec3 c = vec3(led.s, led.dist, 0.0);", "related": ["Led", "shade", "led.pos", "led.s"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "imu", "kind": "context", "category": "input", "scope": "any", "sigs": [], "ty": "", "summary": "inertial context when present", "doc": "The fixture's raw IMU readings when the host drives them (`imu.accel`, `imu.gyro`). For motion effects prefer the sensor uniforms the firmware writes by name, such as `uniform vec3 imu_gravity`, `imu_shake` and `imu_tap`, which also work as ordinary knobs in the preview.", "example": "float shake = length(imu.gyro);", "related": ["imu.accel", "imu.gyro", "uniform"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "room", "kind": "context", "category": "input", "scope": "any", "sigs": [], "ty": "", "summary": "the shared room frame's bounding box (room.min, room.max)", "doc": "The bounding box of the room the fixture is posed in (the host's box, else this fixture's own room-frame box). Together with `led.room` it lets several fixtures render one room-scale effect: normalise `(led.room - room.min) / (room.max - room.min)`.", "example": "vec3 n = (led.room - room.min) / max(room.max - room.min, vec3(0.001));", "related": ["room.min", "room.max", "led.room"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "led.pos", "kind": "member", "category": "input", "scope": "any", "sigs": [], "ty": "vec3", "summary": "LED position in the gravity-leveled, roughly unit-box map frame", "doc": "The LED's position in the fixture's map frame, in metres, +Y up. Use it for spatial patterns; `fixed(led.pos.y)` loads a component as fixed point directly.", "example": "float h = smoothstep(-0.5, 0.5, led.pos.y);", "related": ["led.room", "led.uv"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "led.idx", "kind": "member", "category": "input", "scope": "any", "sigs": [], "ty": "float", "summary": "this LED's index (a float holding an integer; buffer[led.idx] converts it)", "doc": "The LED's index, 0..led.count-1, typed float. Use it to index buffers (`trail[led.idx]`, which converts it to int) or as a per-LED seed (`hash(float(led.idx))`).", "example": "float r = hash(led.idx);", "related": ["led.count", "buffer"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "led.count", "kind": "member", "category": "input", "scope": "any", "sigs": [], "ty": "float", "summary": "total LED count (float)", "doc": "The number of live LEDs on the fixture, typed float. `led.idx / led.count` is a 0..1 ramp in wiring order.", "example": "float t = led.idx / led.count;", "related": ["led.idx"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "led.seg", "kind": "member", "category": "topology", "scope": "any", "sigs": [], "ty": "float", "summary": "topology segment id, -1 if none (float)", "doc": "The id of the topology segment the LED lies on, typed float, -1 when the map has no topology. Pair it with `seg_len` and the other graph queries.", "example": "float hue = fract(led.seg * 0.17);", "related": ["led.s", "seg_len", "seg_count"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "led.s", "kind": "member", "category": "topology", "scope": "any", "sigs": [], "ty": "float", "summary": "0..1 arclength along the LED's segment", "doc": "How far along its segment the LED sits, 0 at endpoint a and 1 at endpoint b. The natural coordinate for chases along a strip: `fract(led.s - ph)`.", "example": "float band = smoothstep(0.1, 0.0, abs(led.s - 0.5));", "related": ["led.seg", "led.dist"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "led.dist", "kind": "member", "category": "topology", "scope": "any", "sigs": [], "ty": "float", "summary": "0..1 geodesic distance from the current source (the topology root by default; call flood_from(node) in update() to re-source it, e.g. to flood from a random endpoint)", "doc": "The distance along the topology graph from the current source to this LED, normalised to 0..1. The source is the topology root until update() calls `flood_from(node)`. Good for floods, ripples and anything that should follow the wiring rather than space.", "example": "float front = step(led.dist, fract(time * 0.2));", "related": ["flood_from", "led.s", "term"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "led.branch", "kind": "member", "category": "topology", "scope": "any", "sigs": [], "ty": "bool", "summary": "true at a junction/branch point", "doc": "True for an LED at a junction of the topology graph, where more than two segments meet.", "example": "vec3 c = vec3(0.0);\nif (led.branch) { c = vec3(1.0); }", "related": ["node_deg", "led.seg"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "led.uv", "kind": "member", "category": "texture", "scope": "any", "sigs": [], "ty": "vec2", "summary": "per-LED texture coord in 0..1 (this LED's XY position normalized over the map bounds — a top-down projection); feed sample(tex, led.uv)", "doc": "A 2D coordinate in 0..1: the LED's map position projected top-down and normalised over the map's bounds. Feed it to `sample(tex, led.uv)` for texture-mapped and pixel-space effects.", "example": "vec2 d = led.uv - vec2(0.5);\nfloat ring = step(dot(d, d), 0.1);", "related": ["sample", "texture", "led.pos"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "led.room", "kind": "member", "category": "input", "scope": "any", "sigs": [], "ty": "vec3", "summary": "LED position in the shared room frame, metres (== led.pos while the fixture is unposed)", "doc": "The LED's position in the shared room frame, after the fixture's room pose (translation, rotation, scale) is applied. It equals `led.pos` until the fixture is posed. Use it with `room.min` / `room.max` so several fixtures render one continuous effect.", "example": "float h = smoothstep(room.min.y, room.max.y, led.room.y);", "related": ["room", "led.pos"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "room.min", "kind": "member", "category": "input", "scope": "any", "sigs": [], "ty": "vec3", "summary": "the room bounding box's minimum corner (vec3)", "doc": "The minimum corner of the room's bounding box, in the room frame.", "example": "vec3 lo = room.min;", "related": ["room.max", "led.room"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "room.max", "kind": "member", "category": "input", "scope": "any", "sigs": [], "ty": "vec3", "summary": "the room bounding box's maximum corner (vec3)", "doc": "The maximum corner of the room's bounding box, in the room frame.", "example": "vec3 hi = room.max;", "related": ["room.min", "led.room"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "imu.accel", "kind": "member", "category": "input", "scope": "any", "sigs": [], "ty": "vec3", "summary": "accelerometer, m/s^2 (when present)", "doc": "The raw accelerometer reading when the host drives the IMU context. For tilt, `uniform vec3 imu_gravity` (written by the firmware's sensor core) is usually easier.", "example": "float g = length(imu.accel);", "related": ["imu.gyro", "imu"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "imu.gyro", "kind": "member", "category": "input", "scope": "any", "sigs": [], "ty": "vec3", "summary": "gyroscope, rad/s (when present)", "doc": "The raw gyroscope reading in rad/s when the host drives the IMU context.", "example": "float spin = length(imu.gyro);", "related": ["imu.accel", "imu"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "sin", "kind": "builtin", "category": "math", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["sin(x)"], "ty": "", "summary": "sine of x (radians); on a fixed arg the angle is in TURNS (1.0 = full circle) and it is an integer LUT, ~4x cheaper", "doc": "The sine of x, component-wise on vectors. A float argument is in radians (`sin(ph * 6.2832)` for a phase in turns). A `fixed`, `fixed16` or `fixed8` argument takes the angle in TURNS (1.0 = one full circle) and runs as an integer lookup table, about four times cheaper. Drive it from an integrated phase, not `time * speed`.", "example": "uniform float rate : 0.1 .. 2.0 = 0.5;\nstate fixed ph;\nvoid update() { ph = fract(ph + fixed(dt * rate)); }\nvec3 shade(Led led) {\n  fixed w = sin(fixed(led.s) + ph);\n  return vec3(float(w) * 0.5 + 0.5);\n}", "related": ["cos", "tan", "atan2", "fixed"], "cost": "float ~570 cycles; fixed (turns) ~150", "constant": true},
  {"name": "cos", "kind": "builtin", "category": "math", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["cos(x)"], "ty": "", "summary": "cosine of x (radians); on a fixed arg: TURNS, integer LUT", "doc": "The cosine of x, component-wise. Radians for a float argument; TURNS and an integer lookup table for a fixed argument, like `sin`.", "example": "fixed c = cos(fixed(led.s));", "related": ["sin", "atan2"], "cost": "float ~650 cycles; fixed (turns) ~150", "constant": true},
  {"name": "tan", "kind": "builtin", "category": "math", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["tan(x)"], "ty": "", "summary": "tangent of x (radians); on a fixed arg: TURNS", "doc": "The tangent of x. Radians for a float argument; a fixed scalar argument takes TURNS and uses an integer path.", "example": "float t = tan(led.s * 0.7);", "related": ["sin", "cos", "atan"], "cost": "float ~1,300 cycles; fixed ~370-480", "constant": true},
  {"name": "abs", "kind": "builtin", "category": "math", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["abs(x)"], "ty": "", "summary": "absolute value", "doc": "The absolute value, component-wise. Native on int and every fixed type.", "example": "float d = abs(led.s - 0.5);", "related": ["sign"], "cost": "int/fixed ~100-150 cycles", "constant": true},
  {"name": "floor", "kind": "builtin", "category": "math", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["floor(x)"], "ty": "", "summary": "largest integer <= x", "doc": "Rounds down to a whole number, component-wise; the result keeps the argument's type. Native on fixed types. With `hash` it makes stepped randomness: `hash(floor(led.s * 8.0))`.", "example": "float cell = floor(led.s * 8.0);", "related": ["ceil", "fract"], "cost": "fixed ~100-150 cycles; float ~260", "constant": true},
  {"name": "ceil", "kind": "builtin", "category": "math", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["ceil(x)"], "ty": "", "summary": "smallest integer >= x", "doc": "Rounds up to a whole number, component-wise. Native on fixed types.", "example": "float c = ceil(led.s * 4.0);", "related": ["floor"], "cost": "fixed ~100-150 cycles; float ~260", "constant": true},
  {"name": "fract", "kind": "builtin", "category": "math", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["fract(x)"], "ty": "", "summary": "fractional part, x - floor(x)", "doc": "The fractional part, `x - floor(x)`, in 0..1. The standard wrap for phases (`ph = fract(ph + dt * speed)`) and repeating patterns. Native on fixed types.", "example": "uniform float speed : 0.0 .. 2.0 = 0.5;\nstate fixed ph;\nvoid update() { ph = fract(ph + fixed(dt * speed)); }\nvec3 shade(Led led) { return hsv2rgb(fract(fixed(led.s) + ph), 0.9fx, 1.0fx); }", "related": ["floor", "mod"], "cost": "fixed ~100-150 cycles; float ~340", "constant": true},
  {"name": "sqrt", "kind": "builtin", "category": "math", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["sqrt(x)"], "ty": "", "summary": "square root", "doc": "The square root, component-wise. A fixed scalar argument uses an integer path. To test a distance, compare squared lengths (`dot(d, d) < r * r`) and skip the root.", "example": "float r = sqrt(led.s);", "related": ["length", "pow"], "cost": "float ~680 cycles; fixed ~480", "constant": true},
  {"name": "exp", "kind": "builtin", "category": "math", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["exp(x)"], "ty": "", "summary": "e raised to the power x (a fixed arg is an integer LUT covering only -2..0.69)", "doc": "e to the power x, component-wise. A fixed argument runs as an integer lookup table but saturates: it covers only -2..0.69. For decays per LED prefer a polynomial or `x * x`.", "example": "float fall = exp(-4.0 * led.s);", "related": ["log", "pow"], "cost": "float ~850 cycles; fixed ~170", "constant": true},
  {"name": "log", "kind": "builtin", "category": "math", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["log(x)"], "ty": "", "summary": "natural logarithm", "doc": "The natural logarithm, component-wise. A fixed scalar argument uses an integer path.", "example": "float l = log(1.0 + led.s);", "related": ["exp", "pow"], "cost": "float ~850 cycles; fixed ~370-480", "constant": true},
  {"name": "sign", "kind": "builtin", "category": "math", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["sign(x)"], "ty": "", "summary": "-1, 0, or 1 by the sign of x", "doc": "-1, 0 or 1 by the sign of x, component-wise. Native on int and fixed types.", "example": "float s = sign(led.pos.x);", "related": ["abs", "step"], "cost": "int/fixed ~100-150 cycles", "constant": true},
  {"name": "min", "kind": "builtin", "category": "math", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["min(a, b)"], "ty": "", "summary": "component-wise minimum", "doc": "The smaller of a and b, component-wise; both must have the same width. Native when both are the same int/fixed type.", "example": "float m = min(led.s, 0.5);", "related": ["max", "clamp"], "cost": "int/fixed ~100-150 cycles", "constant": true},
  {"name": "max", "kind": "builtin", "category": "math", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["max(a, b)"], "ty": "", "summary": "component-wise maximum", "doc": "The larger of a and b, component-wise; both must have the same width. Native when both are the same int/fixed type.", "example": "float m = max(led.s - 0.2, 0.0);", "related": ["min", "clamp"], "cost": "int/fixed ~100-150 cycles", "constant": true},
  {"name": "pow", "kind": "builtin", "category": "math", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["pow(x, y)"], "ty": "", "summary": "x raised to the power y", "doc": "x to the power y, component-wise. It is the most expensive float built-in: per LED write `x * x` (or a polynomial) instead. Two arguments of the same fixed type take an integer path that saturates like `exp`.", "example": "float g = led.s * led.s;   // instead of pow(led.s, 2.0)", "related": ["exp", "sqrt"], "cost": "float ~1,800 cycles; fixed ~720", "constant": true},
  {"name": "mod", "kind": "builtin", "category": "math", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["mod(x, y)"], "ty": "", "summary": "modulo (x - y*floor(x/y))", "doc": "GLSL modulo, `x - y * floor(x / y)`, component-wise; the result has y's sign. Native when both are the same int/fixed type.", "example": "float m = mod(led.idx, 4.0);", "related": ["fract", "floor"], "cost": "int/fixed ~100-150 cycles; float ~590", "constant": true},
  {"name": "step", "kind": "builtin", "category": "math", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["step(edge, x)"], "ty": "", "summary": "0 if x < edge, else 1", "doc": "0 where x is below edge and 1 otherwise, component-wise: a branch-free threshold. Native on the same int/fixed type.", "example": "float on = step(0.5, led.s);", "related": ["smoothstep", "clamp"], "cost": "int/fixed ~100-150 cycles", "constant": true},
  {"name": "atan2", "kind": "builtin", "category": "math", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["atan2(y, x)"], "ty": "", "summary": "angle of the vector (x, y) in radians; with two args of the same fixed type: in TURNS (-0.5..0.5), integer path", "doc": "The angle of the vector (x, y): -π..π radians for float arguments. With both arguments the same fixed type it returns TURNS (-0.5..0.5) on an integer path, ready for a fixed `sin`/`cos` or `hsv2rgb` hue. In shade() of `led.pos` alone it is per-LED invariant, so the compiler caches it.", "example": "fixed ang = atan2(fixed(led.pos.z), fixed(led.pos.x)) + 0.5fx;", "related": ["atan", "sin", "cos"], "cost": "float ~1,040 cycles; fixed (turns) ~350", "constant": true},
  {"name": "atan", "kind": "builtin", "category": "math", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["atan(y)", "atan(y, x)"], "ty": "", "summary": "arc tangent: atan(y) = atan2(y, 1.0); atan(y, x) is atan2", "doc": "GLSL's spelling of the arc tangent. One argument is the principal value `atan2(y, 1.0)`; two arguments are exactly `atan2(y, x)`.", "example": "float a = atan(led.pos.y, led.pos.x);", "related": ["atan2", "tan"], "cost": "as atan2: float ~1,040 cycles", "constant": false},
  {"name": "clamp", "kind": "builtin", "category": "vector", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["clamp(x, lo, hi)"], "ty": "", "summary": "constrain x to the range [lo, hi]", "doc": "Limits x to [lo, hi], component-wise; lo and hi may be scalars against a vector x. Native when x, lo and hi are the same int/fixed type. In fixed point `clamp((x - a) * k, 0.0fx, 1.0fx)` is a cheap ramp that replaces smoothstep.", "example": "float t = clamp(led.s * 2.0 - 0.5, 0.0, 1.0);", "related": ["min", "max", "smoothstep"], "cost": "int/fixed ~100-150 cycles", "constant": true},
  {"name": "mix", "kind": "builtin", "category": "vector", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["mix(a, b, t)"], "ty": "", "summary": "linear interpolation a*(1-t) + b*t (t scalar)", "doc": "Linear interpolation from a (t = 0) to b (t = 1); a and b may be vectors, t is a scalar. Native when a, b and t are the same fixed type.", "example": "vec3 c = mix(vec3(1.0, 0.2, 0.0), vec3(0.0, 0.3, 1.0), led.s);", "related": ["smoothstep", "clamp"], "cost": "fixed ~100-150 cycles; float pays every lane", "constant": true},
  {"name": "smoothstep", "kind": "builtin", "category": "vector", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["smoothstep(lo, hi, x)"], "ty": "", "summary": "smooth Hermite 0..1 ramp between lo and hi", "doc": "A smooth 0..1 ramp as x goes from lo to hi (Hermite `t*t*(3-2t)`); lo > hi ramps down. Always float: per LED in fixed maths a clamped linear ramp is much cheaper.", "example": "float band = smoothstep(0.2, 0.0, abs(led.s - 0.5));", "related": ["step", "clamp", "mix"], "cost": "~700 cycles per lane (always float)", "constant": true},
  {"name": "length", "kind": "builtin", "category": "vector", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["length(v)"], "ty": "", "summary": "Euclidean length of v", "doc": "The Euclidean length of a vector (or |x| of a scalar). Always float. For a radius test compare `dot(d, d)` with `r * r` instead.", "example": "float r = length(led.pos);", "related": ["distance", "dot", "normalize"], "cost": "length(vec3) ~1,000 cycles (always float)", "constant": true},
  {"name": "distance", "kind": "builtin", "category": "vector", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["distance(a, b)"], "ty": "", "summary": "distance between points a and b", "doc": "The Euclidean distance between two points, `length(a - b)`. Always float.", "example": "float d = distance(led.pos, vec3(0.0, 0.5, 0.0));", "related": ["length", "dot"], "cost": "distance(vec3) ~1,200 cycles (always float)", "constant": true},
  {"name": "dot", "kind": "builtin", "category": "vector", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["dot(a, b)"], "ty": "", "summary": "dot product (scalar)", "doc": "The dot product of two vectors of equal width, a scalar. The cheap way to project onto an axis or a plane and to compare squared distances.", "example": "float side = dot(led.pos, vec3(0.0, 1.0, 0.0));", "related": ["cross", "length"], "cost": "dot(vec3) ~440 cycles (always float)", "constant": true},
  {"name": "cross", "kind": "builtin", "category": "vector", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["cross(a, b)"], "ty": "", "summary": "cross product of two vec3s", "doc": "The cross product of two vec3s: a vector perpendicular to both. Always float.", "example": "vec3 n = cross(led.pos, vec3(0.0, 1.0, 0.0));", "related": ["dot", "normalize"], "cost": "~720 cycles (always float)", "constant": true},
  {"name": "normalize", "kind": "builtin", "category": "vector", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["normalize(v)"], "ty": "", "summary": "v scaled to unit length", "doc": "The vector scaled to length 1. Always float, and one of the dearer vector built-ins: normalise once in update() where you can.", "example": "vec3 dir = normalize(led.pos + vec3(0.001));", "related": ["length", "dot"], "cost": "normalize(vec3) ~1,600 cycles (always float)", "constant": true},
  {"name": "transpose", "kind": "builtin", "category": "matrix", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["transpose(m)"], "ty": "", "summary": "the transpose of a mat3 or mat4", "doc": "Swaps rows and columns of a mat3 or mat4. For a pure rotation the transpose is the inverse, which is how a world-frame plane or direction moves into the fixture frame once per frame in update().", "example": "mat3 r = mat3(1.0);\nmat3 inv = transpose(r);", "related": ["mat3", "mat4", "transform"], "cost": "a copy of 9 or 16 floats; do it in update()", "constant": true},
  {"name": "transform", "kind": "builtin", "category": "matrix", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["transform(mat4 m, vec3 p)", "transform(mat3 m, vec2 p)"], "ty": "", "summary": "affine point transform: transform(mat4, vec3) = (m * vec4(p, 1.0)).xyz; transform(mat3, vec2) in 2D", "doc": "Applies an affine matrix to a point: rotation and scale plus translation, without the projective row. `transform(m, p)` with a mat4 and a vec3 equals `(m * vec4(p, 1.0)).xyz` at 9 multiplies + 9 adds instead of 16 + 12; with a mat3 and a vec2 it is the 2D equivalent. A direction (no translation) is `mat3(m) * d`.", "example": "uniform mat4 pose;\nvec3 shade(Led led) {\n  vec3 p = transform(pose, led.pos);\n  return vec3(step(0.0, p.y));\n}", "related": ["mat4", "mat3", "transpose"], "cost": "9 float multiplies + 9 adds (~3,500 cycles) per call", "constant": true},
  {"name": "hash", "kind": "builtin", "category": "noise", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["hash(x)", "hash(v3)"], "ty": "", "summary": "deterministic float hash in [0,1); accepts a float or a vec3 (always float maths)", "doc": "A deterministic pseudo-random value in [0, 1) from a float or a vec3: the same input always gives the same output, on the device and in the preview. Seed it with `float(led.idx)`, a cell (`floor(...)`) or `frame`. Value noise is built from hashes at lattice points mixed together; that is expensive per LED, so precompute or use a fixed-point lattice.", "example": "float sparkle = step(0.97, hash(float(led.idx) + floor(time * 10.0)));", "related": ["floor", "frame", "mix"], "cost": "~185 cycles; 3-D value noise from 8 hashes + mixes ~9,000", "constant": true},
  {"name": "hsv2rgb", "kind": "builtin", "category": "colour", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["hsv2rgb(h, s, v)", "hsv2rgb(hsv)"], "ty": "", "summary": "HSV to linear RGB (vec3), hue 0..1; also accepts a single vec3. Three args of the same fixed type take the integer path (return that colour directly from shade())", "doc": "Converts hue, saturation and value (all 0..1; the hue is not in degrees) to a linear RGB vec3. A full rainbow is `hsv2rgb(fract(t), 1.0, 1.0)`. With three arguments of the same fixed type it runs on the integer path and produces a fixed colour, which must be returned directly from shade() (it cannot be mixed into float maths).", "example": "uniform float speed : 0.0 .. 2.0 = 0.3;\nstate fixed ph;\nvoid update() { ph = fract(ph + fixed(dt * speed)); }\nvec3 shade(Led led) { return hsv2rgb(fract(fixed(led.dist) + ph), 0.9fx, 1.0fx); }", "related": ["rgb", "palette0", "fixed"], "cost": "float ~1,300 cycles; fixed (integer colour) ~370", "constant": true},
  {"name": "rgb", "kind": "builtin", "category": "colour", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["rgb(r, g, b)"], "ty": "", "summary": "a float-free colour from 3 channels of the same fixed type (0..1); return it directly from shade()", "doc": "Builds an integer colour from three channels of the same fixed type (`fixed`, `fixed16` or `fixed8`, each 0..1). It never touches soft-float: shade() returns it as scaled integers. Like the other fixed colours it must be the value of shade()'s `return`; it cannot be stored or mixed.", "example": "vec3 shade(Led led) {\n  fixed t = fixed(led.s);\n  return rgb(t, 0.2fx, 1.0fx - t);\n}", "related": ["rgb8", "hsv2rgb", "fixed"], "cost": "no soft-float: the channels are returned as integers", "constant": false},
  {"name": "rgb8", "kind": "builtin", "category": "colour", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["rgb8(r, g, b)"], "ty": "", "summary": "a float-free colour from 3 int channels in 0..255; return it directly from shade()", "doc": "Builds a colour from three int channels in 0..255, returned from shade() without any float maths. Use it when the colour is already integer data (a palette table, a counter). It must be the value of shade()'s `return`.", "example": "vec3 shade(Led led) {\n  int k = int(led.s * 255.0);\n  return rgb8(k, 0, 255 - k);\n}", "related": ["rgb", "int"], "cost": "no soft-float: the channels are returned as integers", "constant": false},
  {"name": "palette0", "kind": "builtin", "category": "colour", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["palette0(t)"], "ty": "", "summary": "sample built-in palette 0 (fire: black, red, orange, yellow, white) at t (vec3)", "doc": "Samples the built-in fire palette at t in 0..1 (clamped): black → red → orange → yellow → white. A fixed argument takes the integer path and gives a fixed colour to return directly from shade().", "example": "vec3 c = palette0(led.s);", "related": ["palette1", "palette2", "hsv2rgb"], "cost": "float ~500 cycles", "constant": true},
  {"name": "palette1", "kind": "builtin", "category": "colour", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["palette1(t)"], "ty": "", "summary": "sample built-in palette 1 (ice: black, blue, cyan, white) at t (vec3)", "doc": "Samples the built-in ice palette at t in 0..1 (clamped): black → blue → cyan → white. A fixed argument takes the integer path.", "example": "vec3 c = palette1(led.s);", "related": ["palette0", "palette2"], "cost": "float ~500 cycles", "constant": true},
  {"name": "palette2", "kind": "builtin", "category": "colour", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["palette2(t)"], "ty": "", "summary": "sample built-in palette 2 (rainbow) at t (vec3)", "doc": "Samples the built-in rainbow palette at t in 0..1 (clamped); the same as `hsv2rgb(t, 1.0, 1.0)`. A fixed argument takes the integer path.", "example": "vec3 c = palette2(led.s);", "related": ["palette0", "palette1", "hsv2rgb"], "cost": "float ~500 cycles", "constant": true},
  {"name": "palette", "kind": "builtin", "category": "colour", "scope": "reserved", "sigs": ["palette(id, t)"], "ty": "", "summary": "reserved: use palette0(t), palette1(t) or palette2(t)", "doc": "A palette chosen by a runtime id is not supported yet: the compiler reports an error pointing at the fixed-id forms `palette0` (fire), `palette1` (ice) and `palette2` (rainbow).", "example": "vec3 c = palette0(led.s);", "related": ["palette0", "palette1", "palette2"], "cost": "", "constant": true},
  {"name": "sample", "kind": "builtin", "category": "texture", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["sample(tex, uv)"], "ty": "", "summary": "bilinearly sample 2D texture `tex` at uv (vec2, 0..1, edge-clamped); returns the texture's element type", "doc": "Reads a texture declared with `texture`, bilinearly filtered and edge-clamped, at uv in 0..1. The first argument is the texture's name. The result is a float scalar/vector with the element's component count, whatever the storage precision.", "example": "texture vec3 img(8, 8);\nvec3 shade(Led led) { return sample(img, led.uv); }", "related": ["paint", "texture", "led.uv"], "cost": "~3,800 cycles", "constant": false},
  {"name": "paint", "kind": "builtin", "category": "texture", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["paint(tex, uv, color)"], "ty": "", "summary": "write `color` to the nearest texel of `tex` at uv (vec2, 0..1); a void statement", "doc": "Writes a value into the texel of `tex` nearest uv (0..1). It is a statement, not an expression. Painting in update() and sampling in shade() gives drawings, trails and feedback effects that persist across frames.", "example": "texture float ink(16, 16);\nvoid update() { paint(ink, vec2(hash(frame), hash(frame + 1.0)), 1.0); }\nvec3 shade(Led led) { return vec3(sample(ink, led.uv)); }", "related": ["sample", "texture"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "seg_count", "kind": "builtin", "category": "topology", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["seg_count()"], "ty": "", "summary": "number of topology segments (int)", "doc": "The number of segments in the fixture's topology graph.", "example": "int n = seg_count();", "related": ["seg_len", "led.seg"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "seg_len", "kind": "builtin", "category": "topology", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["seg_len(seg)"], "ty": "", "summary": "arclength of segment `seg` in meters (float)", "doc": "The length of segment `seg` (an int id) in metres. Multiply by `led.s` for the distance along it.", "example": "float m = seg_len(int(led.seg)) * led.s;", "related": ["seg_count", "led.s"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "seg_node", "kind": "builtin", "category": "topology", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["seg_node(seg, side)"], "ty": "", "summary": "endpoint node id of `seg` (side 0=a, 1=b; int, -1 if free)", "doc": "The node at one end of a segment: side 0 is endpoint a (where `led.s` is 0), side 1 is endpoint b. -1 when that end is free.", "example": "int a = seg_node(0, 0);", "related": ["node_deg", "node_seg"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "node_deg", "kind": "builtin", "category": "topology", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["node_deg(node)"], "ty": "", "summary": "number of segments incident to `node` (int)", "doc": "How many segments meet at a node: 1 at a terminus, 2 along a path, 3 or more at a junction.", "example": "int d = node_deg(seg_node(0, 1));", "related": ["node_seg", "term"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "node_seg", "kind": "builtin", "category": "topology", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["node_seg(node, k)"], "ty": "", "summary": "k-th segment incident to `node` (int segment id) — pick a branch", "doc": "The k-th segment touching a node (k in 0..node_deg-1). Agents walking the graph use it to pick the next branch.", "example": "int s = node_seg(term(0), 0);", "related": ["node_side", "node_deg"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "node_side", "kind": "builtin", "category": "topology", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["node_side(node, k)"], "ty": "", "summary": "which end (0=a,1=b) of that k-th segment touches `node` (int)", "doc": "For the k-th segment at a node, which of its ends (0 = a, 1 = b) touches the node, so an agent knows whether to walk `led.s` up or down.", "example": "int side = node_side(term(0), 0);", "related": ["node_seg", "seg_node"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "term_count", "kind": "builtin", "category": "topology", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["term_count()"], "ty": "", "summary": "number of termini — degree-1 endpoints of the topology (int)", "doc": "The number of termini: nodes with exactly one segment (the free ends of the strips).", "example": "int n = term_count();", "related": ["term", "flood_from"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "term", "kind": "builtin", "category": "topology", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["term(k)"], "ty": "", "summary": "the k-th terminus node id (int, -1 if out of range); pick a random one to flood/spawn from", "doc": "The node id of the k-th terminus, -1 out of range. Pick one at random to start a flood or spawn an agent.", "example": "int t = term(int(hash(frame) * float(term_count())));", "related": ["term_count", "flood_from"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "flood_from", "kind": "builtin", "category": "topology", "scope": "update", "sigs": ["flood_from(node)"], "ty": "", "summary": "set the geodesic source: `led.dist` then reports distance from `node` (0..1). Call once per cycle in update() — lets Flood/agents start from any endpoint. void", "doc": "Re-roots `led.dist`: after the call it measures the graph distance from `node`. Call it from update(), once per cycle (for example when a wavefront finishes), not every frame or per LED. A void statement.", "example": "uniform float speed : 0.1 .. 2.0 = 0.5;\nstate float front;\nvoid update() {\n  front = front + dt * speed;\n  if (front > 1.0) {\n    front = 0.0;\n    flood_from(term(int(hash(frame) * float(term_count()))));\n  }\n}\nvec3 shade(Led led) { return vec3(step(led.dist, front) * step(front - 0.2, led.dist)); }", "related": ["led.dist", "term", "term_count"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "fired", "kind": "builtin", "category": "trigger", "scope": "update", "sigs": ["fired(trigger)"], "ty": "", "summary": "true once per event of a `uniform trigger` (update() only)", "doc": "True in the frame after a `uniform trigger` received an event (a tap, a button press), false otherwise. Only valid in update() / init(): a trigger fires once per frame, not per LED. The argument is the trigger's name.", "example": "uniform trigger tap;\nstate float hue;\nvoid update() { if (fired(tap)) { hue = fract(hue + 0.25); } }\nvec3 shade(Led led) { return hsv2rgb(hue, 0.9, 1.0); }", "related": ["trigger", "fire"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "fire", "kind": "builtin", "category": "trigger", "scope": "driver", "sigs": ["fire(trigger)"], "ty": "", "summary": "bump an `export trigger` from a driver's poll() (one event)", "doc": "Raises one event on an `export trigger` declared by a sensor driver. Effects that declare a `uniform trigger` of the same name see `fired(...)` once. A void statement.", "example": "export trigger tap;\nstate int last;\nvoid poll() {\n  int now = gpio_read(9);\n  if (now == 0 && last == 1) { fire(tap); }\n  last = now;\n}", "related": ["fired", "trigger", "export"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "gpio_read", "kind": "builtin", "category": "driver", "scope": "driver", "sigs": ["gpio_read(pin)"], "ty": "", "summary": "read a GPIO pin in a driver's poll(): 0 / 1 (-1 when unavailable)", "doc": "Reads a GPIO pin's level in a sensor driver: 0 or 1, -1 when the pin is unavailable. The pin is an int. Outside a driver's poll() there is no hardware and it returns -1.", "example": "export float button;\nvoid poll() { button = float(gpio_read(9)); }", "related": ["gpio_mode", "poll", "fire"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "gpio_mode", "kind": "builtin", "category": "driver", "scope": "driver", "sigs": ["gpio_mode(pin, mode)"], "ty": "", "summary": "configure a GPIO input in a driver: mode 0 input, 1 pull-up, 2 pull-down; returns 1 when applied", "doc": "Configures a pin as an input in a sensor driver: mode 0 plain input, 1 with pull-up, 2 with pull-down. Returns 1 when applied. Both arguments are ints.", "example": "export float button;\nvoid poll() {\n  int ok = gpio_mode(9, 1);\n  button = float(gpio_read(9));\n}", "related": ["gpio_read", "poll"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "i2c_write", "kind": "builtin", "category": "driver", "scope": "driver", "sigs": ["i2c_write(addr, reg, byte)"], "ty": "", "summary": "write one byte to an I2C register in a driver's poll(); returns the ACK (1/0)", "doc": "Writes `byte` to register `reg` of the I2C device at 7-bit address `addr`, in a sensor driver. Returns 1 on ACK, 0 otherwise. All arguments are ints. Outside poll() there is no bus.", "example": "export float ok;\nvoid poll() { ok = float(i2c_write(104, 107, 0)); }", "related": ["i2c_read8", "poll"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "i2c_read8", "kind": "builtin", "category": "driver", "scope": "driver", "sigs": ["i2c_read8(addr, reg)"], "ty": "", "summary": "read one byte from an I2C register in a driver (int, -1 on failure)", "doc": "Reads one byte from register `reg` of the I2C device at `addr` in a sensor driver: 0..255, or -1 when the read fails or there is no bus.", "example": "export float who;\nvoid poll() { who = float(i2c_read8(104, 117)); }", "related": ["i2c_read16", "i2c_write"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "i2c_read16", "kind": "builtin", "category": "driver", "scope": "driver", "sigs": ["i2c_read16(addr, reg)"], "ty": "", "summary": "read a big-endian unsigned 16-bit word from an I2C register in a driver", "doc": "Reads two bytes starting at register `reg` as a big-endian unsigned word (0..65535), -1 on failure.", "example": "export float raw;\nvoid poll() { raw = float(i2c_read16(64, 0)); }", "related": ["i2c_read16s", "i2c_read8"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "i2c_read16s", "kind": "builtin", "category": "driver", "scope": "driver", "sigs": ["i2c_read16s(addr, reg)"], "ty": "", "summary": "read a big-endian signed 16-bit word (MEMS sensor data) from an I2C register in a driver", "doc": "Reads two bytes starting at `reg` as a big-endian signed word (-32768..32767), the format of most MEMS accelerometers and gyroscopes.", "example": "export vec3 accel;\nvoid poll() {\n  accel = vec3(float(i2c_read16s(104, 59)), float(i2c_read16s(104, 61)), float(i2c_read16s(104, 63))) / 16384.0;\n}", "related": ["i2c_read16", "export"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "color", "kind": "annotation", "category": "uniform ui", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["uniform vec3 name : color = r, g, b;", "uniform vec4 name : color = r, g, b, a;"], "ty": "", "summary": "uniform annotation: show a vec3/vec4 as a colour picker (linear RGB 0..1)", "doc": "Shows a vec3/vec4 uniform as a colour picker holding linear RGB in 0..1. It is also the default for an unannotated vec3/vec4. Not valid on a matrix.", "example": "uniform vec3 tint : color = 1.0, 0.4, 0.1;\nvec3 shade(Led led) { return tint * led.s; }", "related": ["uniform", "range", "hsv2rgb"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "vector", "kind": "annotation", "category": "uniform ui", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["uniform vec2 name : vector = x, y;", "uniform vec2 name : vector lo .. hi [step s] = x, y;", "uniform vec3 name : vector vec3(lo..) .. vec3(hi..) = vec3(v);"], "ty": "", "summary": "uniform annotation `: vector [lo .. hi] [step s]`: an XY pad for a vec2 (a vec3 adds a z slider); needs .fxb format 2", "doc": "Shows a vec2 or vec3 uniform as an XY pad (a vec3 adds a z slider) instead of one slider per component. Without a range every axis is -1..1; the optional range and `step s` take the same forms as a slider's, including a per-component `vecN(...) .. vecN(...)`. Only valid on a vec2/vec3. An effect that uses it is stamped `.fxb` format 2, so firmware from before format 2 refuses it; on those players use a plain range.", "example": "uniform vec2 wind : vector -2.0 .. 2.0 = 0.5, 0.0;\nstate vec2 off;\nvoid update() { off = off + wind * dt; }\nvec3 shade(Led led) { return vec3(fract(dot(led.pos.xy, vec2(1.0)) - off.x - off.y)); }", "related": ["uniform", "range", "direction", "vec2", "vec3"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "direction", "kind": "annotation", "category": "uniform ui", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["uniform vec2 name : direction = x, y;", "uniform vec3 name : direction = x, y, z;", "uniform vec3 name : direction;"], "ty": "", "summary": "uniform annotation `: direction`: a unit vec2 (angle dial) or vec3 (aiming sphere); default normalised, +y when omitted; needs .fxb format 2", "doc": "Shows a vec2 uniform as an angle dial and a vec3 as an aiming sphere. The value is always a unit vector: the compiler normalises the default (which must not be zero, and is +y when omitted), and keypoint blends and agent ramps renormalise. Only valid on a vec2/vec3. An effect that uses it is stamped `.fxb` format 2, so firmware from before format 2 refuses it; on those players use a plain range.", "example": "uniform vec3 sun : direction = 0.0, 1.0, 0.3;\nvec3 shade(Led led) { return vec3(max(dot(led.pos, sun), 0.0)); }", "related": ["uniform", "vector", "normalize", "vec3"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "transform2d", "kind": "annotation", "category": "uniform ui", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["uniform mat3 name : transform2d;", "uniform mat3 name : transform2d lo .. hi;"], "ty": "", "summary": "uniform annotation `: transform2d`: a mat3 as a 2D affine transform (Move / Rotate / Scale gizmo); needs .fxb format 2", "doc": "Marks a mat3 uniform as a 2D homogeneous affine transform: column-major, the translation in column 2. The app edits it on a flat gizmo (Move x/y, Rotate about z, Scale), with the raw 3×3 grid behind Advanced. A plain mat3 is a 3D rotation / linear transform instead, and a mat4 is already a full 3D transform, so the annotation is only valid on a mat3. The optional range bounds every element, as for any matrix uniform. It defaults to the identity. An effect that uses it is stamped `.fxb` format 2, so firmware from before format 2 refuses it.", "example": "uniform mat3 uvx : transform2d;\nvec3 shade(Led led) { vec2 p = transform(uvx, led.pos.xy); return vec3(fract(p.x), fract(p.y), 0.5); }", "related": ["uniform", "mat3", "transform", "range"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "range", "kind": "annotation", "category": "uniform ui", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["uniform float name : lo .. hi = v;", "uniform float name : lo .. hi step s = v;", "uniform vec3 name : lo .. hi = x, y, z;", "uniform vec3 name : vec3(lo..) .. vec3(hi..) = vec3(v);", "uniform mat4 name : lo .. hi;"], "ty": "", "summary": "uniform annotation `: lo .. hi` (optionally `step s`): a slider (scalars), numeric per-component sliders (vectors) or a bounded matrix", "doc": "A numeric range makes a slider: `lo .. hi` (either may be negative). The slider has the uniform's native precision: continuous for a float, whole numbers for an int, the least step for a fixed type; `step s` after the range snaps the control (editor, MIDI and keypoint blends) to a grid, and `step 0` asks for continuous explicitly. On a vector one range applies to every component and makes it a numeric vector rather than a colour; `vecN(...) .. vecN(...)` gives each component its own range. On a matrix it bounds every element (the default is ±1000). The uniform's default follows `=`.", "example": "uniform float width : 0.02 .. 0.5 = 0.1;\nuniform float bands : 1.0 .. 8.0 step 1.0 = 3.0;\nuniform vec3 dir : -1.0 .. 1.0 = 0.0, 1.0, 0.0;\nvec3 shade(Led led) { return vec3(step(abs(dot(led.pos, dir)), width) * fract(led.s * bands)); }", "related": ["uniform", "unit", "dropdown"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "dropdown", "kind": "annotation", "category": "uniform ui", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["uniform int name : {\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"} = 0;"], "ty": "", "summary": "uniform annotation `: {\"a\",\"b\"}`: a dropdown whose value is the option index", "doc": "A list of option strings in braces makes a dropdown. The uniform's value is the index of the chosen option, so declare it `int` and compare with `==`.", "example": "uniform int mode : {\"fire\", \"ice\"} = 0;\nvec3 shade(Led led) {\n  vec3 c = palette0(led.s);\n  if (mode == 1) { c = palette1(led.s); }\n  return c;\n}", "related": ["uniform", "int"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "unit", "kind": "annotation", "category": "uniform ui", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["uniform float name : lo .. hi \"unit\" = v;", "export float name \"unit\";"], "ty": "", "summary": "an optional unit string after a uniform's annotation (or an export's name)", "doc": "A string after the annotation (or alone after `:`) names the uniform's unit, shown next to its control and given to agents in the manifest. A sensor driver's `export` takes one after its name.", "example": "uniform float speed : 0.0 .. 4.0 \"Hz\" = 1.0;\nstate float ph;\nvoid update() { ph = fract(ph + dt * speed); }\nvec3 shade(Led led) { return vec3(ph); }", "related": ["uniform", "range", "export"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "storage", "kind": "annotation", "category": "storage", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["buffer vec3 name : fixed8;", "texture vec3 name(w, h) : fixed16;"], "ty": "", "summary": "buffer/texture annotation `: fixed8` / `: fixed16`: store float/vec elements compressed (read and written as float)", "doc": "On a float or vector buffer/texture element, `: fixed8` stores each component in one byte and `: fixed16` in two, values in [-2, 2). Reads and writes still use floats; only the stored precision drops. It quarters (or halves) the element's RAM in the shared 24 KB effect arena.", "example": "buffer vec3 glow : fixed8;\nvec3 shade(Led led) {\n  vec3 g = glow[led.idx] * 0.95;\n  glow[led.idx] = g;\n  return g;\n}", "related": ["buffer", "texture", "fixed8", "fixed16"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "fx", "kind": "syntax", "category": "literal", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["0.5fx", "0.5fx16", "0.5fx8"], "ty": "", "summary": "fixed-point literal suffixes: 0.5fx = fixed(0.5), 0.5fx16 = fixed16(0.5), 0.5fx8 = fixed8(0.5)", "doc": "A numeric literal with the suffix `fx`, `fx16` or `fx8` is a fixed, fixed16 or fixed8 constant, folded at compile time. Use them in fixed-point maths: a float literal there turns the whole expression back into soft-float. `fx16` and `fx8` literals must be below 2.", "example": "fixed x = fixed(led.s) * 0.6fx + 0.1fx;", "related": ["fixed", "fixed16", "fixed8"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "swizzle", "kind": "syntax", "category": "vector", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["v.x", "v.xy", "v.rgb", "v.zyx", "v[i]"], "ty": "", "summary": "vector component access: .x .y .z .w / .r .g .b .a in any order and length, or v[i]", "doc": "Pick or reorder vector components with up to four of `x y z w` (or `r g b a`): `led.pos.xz`, `c.bgr`, `v.xxx`. A vector can also be indexed like an array, `v[1]` being `v.y`.", "example": "vec2 flat = led.pos.xz;\nvec3 p = led.pos;\nfloat y = p[1];", "related": ["vec2", "vec3", "vec4"], "cost": "", "constant": false},
  {"name": "array", "kind": "syntax", "category": "declaration", "scope": "any", "sigs": ["T name[N];", "a[i]"], "ty": "", "summary": "fixed-size arrays `T name[N];` (state or locals), indexed a[i] with a runtime int", "doc": "Any scalar, vector or struct type can form a fixed-size array (1..255 elements, at most 255 slots in total) as state or a local. Index with `a[i]`; a runtime int index is fine. Locals start at zero. There are no array literals: fill arrays in init() or a loop.", "example": "float w[4];\nfor (int i = 0; i < 4; i = i + 1) { w[i] = hash(float(i)); }\nfloat pick = w[2];", "related": ["struct", "for", "state"], "cost": "", "constant": false}
